1. Purpose

Grey M1 solver a la Radice et al. 2022 [arXiv:2111.14858].

This code needs 2 ghost cells on unigrid and 4 ghost cells with AMR.

2. IMEX collision contract and remaining limitations

IMEX42L, IMEX32L, IMEX122, and Implicit Euler always use the nonlinear
diagonal collision residual for E and F with the selected closure. The
source_force_nonlinear option and source_thick_limit/source_scat_limit
shortcuts retain their legacy meaning only for the semi-implicit method.
The IMEX number update always solves its scalar absorption/emission equation
analytically, using Gamma=W*(E-F.v)/J from the accepted physical state without
a transport-floor or rad_eps approximation. The exactly empty radiation
state uses the isotropic current Gamma=W. The zero-diagonal source RHS and
current-stage backreaction use the same physical current. IMEX ignores the
legacy source_therm_limit shortcut. source_limiter must be -1 for IMEX.
The established semi-implicit predictor/corrector is otherwise unchanged.

The componentwise acceptance test requires both the nonlinear residual and
the solver step to be no larger than

  source_epsabs + source_epsrel * max(abs(q_component), abs(qstar_component)).

The reference is the provisional state defining the residual, not the
analytic initial guess or an opacity-amplified source scale. Small fluxes
therefore do not inherit an energy-sized relative error allowance. Choose
source_epsabs in the units and magnitude of the moments actually being
resolved; an absolute tolerance larger than those moments cannot establish
relative accuracy. The same acceptance test applies at every diagonal stage.

For IMEX the stored provisional state is the source baseline, without a
second local floor or realizability repair. In the IMEX42L/32L ODESolvers
stage sequence, PostStep repairs the provisional state before the driver
saves its diagonal baseline. The source kernel commits its physical root
directly, rather than reconstructing it as qstar+(qnew-qstar). The existing
PostStep projection of the accepted state occurs after the IMEX42L/32L driver
extracts the diagonal rate. The older IMEX122/Implicit Euler driver instead
copies its intermediate state after PostStep and can still include that
projection in its recovered implicit rate; nuX does not change that driver.
No additional final implicit projection or change of Butcher coefficients
is introduced. Zero collision rates leave the stored
state untouched. The scalar number state uses a positive-form analytic
solution, while its matter-exchange increment is evaluated without subtracting
two nearly equal number densities.

IMEX closure evaluations do not replace a failed variable closure with an
endpoint tensor, regardless of use_fallback. A failed closure on a Newton
trial returns an invalid-callback status so the trust-region solver can
backtrack. Only a failed actual-state closure or exhausted diagonal solve is
fatal. Outside IMEX the existing use_fallback behavior is retained.

The selected imex_backreaction policy and its default are unchanged.
"implicit" conservatively applies opposite collision increments to matter
conserved variables, but matter primitives are frozen during the radiation
solve: this is not a fully coupled matter/radiation nonlinear solve.
"explicit" keeps the paper's matter split and evaluates its physical source
from the current accepted stage, after primitive/closure/rate refresh.
Both modes still require physical verification in stiff coupled matter tests.

In particular, the radiation solve still freezes opacities evaluated from the
provisional state. Radiation-dependent mean-energy opacity corrections and
trapped-equilibrium prescriptions are not iterated to the accepted radiation
state here. Thus solving the fixed-coefficient residual is not proof that the
fully population-dependent collision equation is solved, or that a separately
refreshed explicit matter source exactly cancels the diagonal radiation rate.
Stiff transport/source cancellation, frozen matter feedback, and the observed
opaque-core TOV flux spotting remain unresolved numerical questions. The
strict solve must not be interpreted as evidence that spotting is cured.

The current local CarpetX/ODESolvers subcycling driver also has a separate
IMEX32L coefficient discrepancy: its last implicit row is (1/6,1/6,2/3),
which sums to 1, whereas the explicit stage abscissa is 1/2. With the stored
weights this gives b.A_I.1=5/6 instead of the second-order condition 1/2.
This nuX-only branch does not modify CarpetX. Correct and independently
validate that driver/table before treating its IMEX32L convergence as verified.

3. Focused checks (added as source/parfile, not executed here)

nuX_Tests/test/unit_tests.par exercises source math, including IMEX policy,
nonstiff/thick/scattering thresholds, independently evaluated diagonal
residuals, separate small-flux residual/step checks, recoverable closure trial
failure, zero number rates, near-equilibrium number exchange, and strongly
absorbing tiny positive energy/number roots and moving below-floor number
currents. These checks require a future
build and test run; adding them is not a passing test result.

par/nuX_M1_imex_relaxation_3D.par is a small, source-only FakeRates case.
It deliberately sets all legacy source thresholds and source_force_nonlinear=no
to check automatic IMEX policy. In the static unit-density background the
electron species obey dE/dt=-100 E and dE/dt=-E, respectively, with N=E;
the heavy species has zero rates and must remain unchanged. Compare each
method's scalar RK stability function, not a diagonal backward-Euler step,
against the whole-step output. Repeat with IMEX32L and with all three legacy
thresholds set to -1; results should agree to roundoff. Refine dt to compare
the absorption solution with the exponential. This does not exercise the
coupled TOV transport/source balance or establish TOV stability.

